{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgRzXfRCzez9Kbyu6PLcF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Connect flow is fixed in the working tree (not committed). I tested it end to end against a local stand-in for Intervals.icu, not the real service, so the scope string still needs a check on a real account before rollout.\n\n**Three causes, all in the code:**\n\n1. **Connect was a GET link, so it always returned 401.** A page navigation doesn't send the SPA's `Authorization` header. Init is now an authenticated `POST` that returns `{authorize_url}`, and the button navigates to it.\n2. **The scope string named `ACTIVITY` and `CALENDAR` twice.** Since calendar sync shipped on 10-02, the authorize step fails. The scope is now `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`. The logged error body is a generic `invalid_request`, so the duplicate-area diagnosis comes from your colleague's note and is not confirmed.\n3. **The server invented a one-hour token expiry.** Intervals sends no `expires_in` and no `refresh_token`, and the refresher marked Rivera's connection dead an hour after connecting, with no revocation on their side. Expiry and refresh are gone; a 401 from the API now clears the token and shows \"reconnect\".\n\n**Colleague's note:** I applied the no-refresh-token handling, the one-entry-per-area scope rule, and the POST init. I did not strip `grant_type`/`redirect_uri` from the token exchange, because the logged exchange with them succeeded. I did not add a JWT with a `purpose` claim (the existing signed state already covers it), an account-mismatch guard, or encryption at rest (needs a key-management decision for the Postgres store). `ANSWER.md` lists each of these.\n\n**Verification:** Before the fix, the GET init returned 401 without the header, and the duplicate-scope authorize was refused. After the fix, POST init returns the authorize URL, the callback stores the connection, wellness returns 200, a revoked token gives 409 with the reconnect status, and reconnect works. The JS passes `node --check`. I did not test the button in a real browser.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- I accidentally sent one GET to the real intervals.icu authorize page, with a dev client ID and a dev-signed state. No data was submitted and I sent nothing further there.\n- Port 8766 is held by another project's server (cwd `/private/tmp/h-kKlOSc`). I didn't touch it and tested on 8767.\n- `static/app.js` falls back to signing in as `tok-okafor` when no token is stored. That's a dev convenience, but it would sign any multi-tenant visitor in as C. Okafor, so remove it before deploying. I left it alone because it's outside this fix.\n\nChanged files: `server.py`, `config.py`, `static/app.js`, plus the new `ANSWER.md`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2613,"cache_read_input_tokens":55247,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2613},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cd80a493-9a40-40c7-b584-f7581df99cea","uuid":"a0a8d56a-2579-4176-854c-ad11cd801a99","timestamp":"2026-10-27T02:58:39.154Z","request_id":"req_011CgRzXf9qG2hWuiSwcU2dE"}
